=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is currently operating correctly. The inter-rack path pc_0_0 → pc_0_1 is functional end-to-end: leaf_router_0_0 has a valid BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_0, and leaf_router_0_1 has a valid BGP route to 10.0.0.0/24 via spine_router_0_0. Ping confirms 0% loss at a stable ~1.2 ms RTT.

The outage reported "since this morning" was caused by a transient failure of spine_router_0_0. The infra team's diagnosis ("a spine box died") was correct. Because no `maximum-paths` directive is configured on any router in the fabric, every leaf router selects a single best path using BGP router-ID tiebreaking — spine_router_0_0 (router-id 172.16.0.1) always wins over spine_router_0_1 (172.16.0.3). Both leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1 have the spine_router_0_1 backup path in their BGP RIBs (confirmed via `show bgp ipv4 unicast`), but it is not installed in the FIB. When spine_router_0_0 went down, the BGP hold timer (90 seconds by default) had to expire before the leaves withdrew the unreachable next-hop and promoted spine_router_0_1's path — during this ~90-second window, all inter-rack traffic was black-holed. Once spine_router_0_0 recovered and re-established its sessions, it immediately reclaimed the best-path role and traffic was restored.

The corrective fix for future resilience is to add `maximum-paths 2` under the `address-family ipv4 unicast` stanza on both leaf routers (and all spine routers), enabling ECMP across both spines and ensuring that a single spine failure causes an instantaneous reroute rather than a 90-second convergence delay. Deploying BFD on the leaf-to-spine links would further reduce the detection window to milliseconds.
=== END ===